1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,582
A garden cable.

2
00:00:01,582 --> 00:00:02,810
A sunflower root.

3
00:00:02,810 --> 00:00:03,845
A maize root.

4
00:00:04,245 --> 00:00:08,548
Cut a cable, and you see layers: a
cover outside, wires in the middle.

5
00:00:08,548 --> 00:00:10,818
Cut a root, and you see layers too.

6
00:00:11,268 --> 00:00:15,532
Aarav pulled a weed by the bicycle
track and asked: a root is thinner than

7
00:00:15,532 --> 00:00:16,432
a pencil.

8
00:00:16,269 --> 00:00:18,307
How does it hold so much inside?

9
00:00:18,657 --> 00:00:23,342
Under the microscope, a root is rings
inside rings, and each ring has a

10
00:00:23,342 --> 00:00:24,242
job.

11
00:00:24,357 --> 00:00:28,773
By the end, you will read a root section
from outside in, and tell a dicot

12
00:00:28,773 --> 00:00:31,094
root from a monocot root at a glance.

13
00:00:31,744 --> 00:00:34,084
First, the four zones, from outside in.

14
00:00:34,434 --> 00:00:36,813
The epiblema: the skin, with root hairs.

15
00:00:36,813 --> 00:00:39,232
The cortex: many layers
of soft parenchyma.

16
00:00:39,682 --> 00:00:44,281
The endodermis: one ring of tightly
packed cells, the innermost layer of

17
00:00:44,281 --> 00:00:45,207
the cortex.

18
00:00:45,657 --> 00:00:48,717
And the stele: everything
inside the endodermis.

19
00:00:48,717 --> 00:00:51,883
The pericycle, the vascular
bundles and the pith.

20
00:00:52,533 --> 00:00:55,868
Let's zoom into a sunflower root, a dicot.

21
00:00:56,218 --> 00:00:59,169
Many cells of the epiblema
stick out as root hairs.

22
00:00:59,169 --> 00:01:01,139
Each root hair is one long cell.

23
00:01:01,639 --> 00:01:03,741
Cut across, and the rings appear.

24
00:01:03,741 --> 00:01:07,625
Below the epiblema, the cortex has
thin-walled cells with spaces between

25
00:01:07,625 --> 00:01:08,525
them.

26
00:01:08,624 --> 00:01:12,732
Then the endodermis: barrel-shaped
cells, packed with no spaces.

27
00:01:12,732 --> 00:01:16,810
Their walls carry a waxy band
of suberin, the casparian strip.

28
00:01:16,810 --> 00:01:18,795
Water cannot pass through it.

29
00:01:19,295 --> 00:01:22,910
Next, the pericycle: a few
layers of thick-walled cells.

30
00:01:22,910 --> 00:01:26,631
Side roots start here, and
later the vascular cambium too.

31
00:01:27,131 --> 00:01:31,781
At the centre: two to four patches
of xylem and phloem, the conjunctive

32
00:01:31,781 --> 00:01:34,491
tissue between them, and a small pith.

33
00:01:35,141 --> 00:01:37,975
Now watch how the xylem
of a root is laid out.

34
00:01:38,325 --> 00:01:42,770
The xylem and phloem patches take
turns around the centre, on different

35
00:01:42,770 --> 00:01:43,670
radii.

36
00:01:43,383 --> 00:01:45,144
This is a radial bundle.

37
00:01:45,894 --> 00:01:48,510
The first xylem to form
is called protoxylem.

38
00:01:48,510 --> 00:01:52,083
In a root, it forms at the
outer edge, near the pericycle.

39
00:01:52,833 --> 00:01:55,218
The later xylem, metaxylem,
forms towards the centre.

40
00:01:55,218 --> 00:01:57,836
Protoxylem outside, metaxylem
inside: this is called exarch.

41
00:01:58,586 --> 00:02:03,577
Later, in a dicot root, a cambium ring
forms between the xylem and phloem,

42
00:02:03,577 --> 00:02:05,679
so the root can grow thicker.

43
00:02:06,429 --> 00:02:08,718
A monocot root never does this.

44
00:02:08,718 --> 00:02:11,865
Monocot roots do not
undergo secondary growth.

45
00:02:12,615 --> 00:02:16,859
Now the pair to tell apart: the
dicot root and the monocot root.

46
00:02:17,209 --> 00:02:22,059
Both have an epidermis, cortex,
endodermis, pericycle, vascular bundles

47
00:02:22,059 --> 00:02:22,959
and pith.

48
00:02:23,385 --> 00:02:27,350
But a dicot root has two to four
xylem patches and a small pith.

49
00:02:27,350 --> 00:02:31,538
A monocot root usually has more than
six, called polyarch, and a large

50
00:02:31,538 --> 00:02:32,438
pith.

51
00:02:32,705 --> 00:02:33,605
Your turn.

52
00:02:33,535 --> 00:02:37,123
A root section shows twelve
xylem patches and a big pith.

53
00:02:37,123 --> 00:02:38,487
Dicot, or monocot?

54
00:02:38,487 --> 00:02:39,708
Pause and decide.

55
00:02:42,908 --> 00:02:43,808
Monocot!

56
00:02:43,604 --> 00:02:47,731
More than six xylem bundles, and
a large, well developed pith.

57
00:02:48,381 --> 00:02:49,410
Remember this.

58
00:02:49,410 --> 00:02:53,856
In a root, xylem and phloem take
turns, and protoxylem faces outward.

59
00:02:54,456 --> 00:02:56,171
Now, a worked example.

60
00:02:56,171 --> 00:02:59,722
Let's sort four clues into
dicot root or monocot root.

61
00:03:00,072 --> 00:03:01,388
Four xylem patches.

62
00:03:01,388 --> 00:03:02,913
That is a dicot root.

63
00:03:03,363 --> 00:03:05,422
A large, well developed pith.

64
00:03:05,422 --> 00:03:07,088
That is a monocot root.

65
00:03:07,538 --> 00:03:09,516
More than six xylem bundles.

66
00:03:09,516 --> 00:03:11,240
That is a monocot root.

67
00:03:11,690 --> 00:03:13,583
A cambium ring forms later.

68
00:03:13,583 --> 00:03:15,176
That is a dicot root.

69
00:03:15,826 --> 00:03:17,287
Now, the exam trap.

70
00:03:17,637 --> 00:03:21,457
Many students think the pericycle
is the last layer of the cortex.

71
00:03:21,857 --> 00:03:25,562
But the endodermis is the
innermost layer of the cortex.

72
00:03:25,562 --> 00:03:29,114
The pericycle lies inside
it, as part of the stele.

73
00:03:29,764 --> 00:03:31,246
Where is this asked?

74
00:03:31,246 --> 00:03:35,979
As statements and diagram labels:
casparian strips, where side roots begin,

75
00:03:35,979 --> 00:03:37,868
and how many xylem bundles.

76
00:03:38,468 --> 00:03:39,641
Exam level now.

77
00:03:39,641 --> 00:03:42,911
Which statement about a
dicot root is not correct?

78
00:03:42,911 --> 00:03:44,133
Pause and solve.

79
00:03:49,533 --> 00:03:50,836
The answer is C.

80
00:03:50,836 --> 00:03:52,542
That statement is wrong.

81
00:03:53,042 --> 00:03:56,228
Root xylem is exarch: its
protoxylem faces the outside.

82
00:03:56,728 --> 00:04:00,005
Endarch xylem, with protoxylem
towards the centre, is found in stems.

83
00:04:00,605 --> 00:04:02,146
Here is your recap.

84
00:04:02,146 --> 00:04:03,730
Screenshot this card.

85
00:04:06,930 --> 00:04:09,116
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

86
00:04:09,516 --> 00:04:10,416
One.

87
00:04:09,922 --> 00:04:12,042
Which layer carries the casparian strips?

88
00:04:12,942 --> 00:04:13,842
Two.

89
00:04:13,358 --> 00:04:15,334
In which layer do side roots begin?

90
00:04:16,234 --> 00:04:17,134
Three.

91
00:04:16,825 --> 00:04:20,366
How many xylem bundles does
a monocot root usually have?

92
00:04:21,466 --> 00:04:26,143
Next time: why do the bundles in a
maize stem lie scattered everywhere?

93
00:04:26,143 --> 00:04:29,490
We find out in anatomy of
dicot and monocot stems.
